    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one thing though, the clause mentions \"unforeseeable and uncontrollable events\" which is pretty vague. in a legal dispute, how would a court typically distinguish between a legitimate force majeure event and something that is just a standard business risk or bad luck?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The distinction in legal terms when distinguishing a legitimate force majeure event from ordinary business risk hinges on the elements of \"unforeseeability,\" \"uncontrollability,\" and \"extrinsicality.\" For an event to be considered a force majeure, it must be unforeseeable at the time of the lease's execution, something that even the most diligent parties could not have anticipated. It must also be uncontrollable—meaning there were no reasonable measures that could have been taken to avoid or mitigate its impact. Finally, the event must be extrinsic, meaning it lies beyond the normal scope of business risks typically faced by both landlord and tenant.\n\n\nA court will closely examine the language of the contract and assess the commercial context to decide if the event genuinely meets these criteria or if it stems from inherent business risks subjects to regular financial management and due diligence. In a dispute, the party claiming a force majeure must generally prove that the event was not only unpredictable and impossible to control but also that such an event fundamentally disrupted their ability to fulfill contractual obligations. Courts will typically look for specific precedent and interpretations of similar precedents to guide their decision-making.",
      "source": "target",
